Reason: a precise collision test scanned the two boxes' whole overlap, sample by sample, until a hit. A power bomb's
blast takes in walls stretched far past its disc and doors whose boxes reach 16 pixels past their sprites, and in
rm_a5c22 those scans took 85% of the blast's samples: misses over door boxes (~640 samples each) and hits found
thousands of samples into a long wall. The first row is still scanned as before (most hits are there); past it, in
overlaps with 384 samples or more, the samples go in blocks of 4 by 4, and a block is passed over when its corner samples, put through the per-sample
arithmetic, can only land outside a side's sprite or on 8 by 8 cells of its mask with no set pixel (rotation and scale
keep every sample between its block's corners; the slack covers truncation onto pixel 0 and float rounding, and
scales under 0.01 are always scanned). The whole overlap is checked the same way first. The answer is the same for
every pair.
Upstream: Speed only, every platform. Retires this patch when merged.

--- a/src/collision.h
+++ b/src/collision.h
